# Text is stored with LF in the repository, whatever each machine's core.autocrlf says. A Windows
# checkout and a macOS checkout then commit byte-identical text, and no file shows as modified just
# because it was opened on the other OS. (Binary files are detected and left alone.)
* text=auto

# Files that are EXECUTED on Linux must keep LF endings, whatever the checkout platform does.
#
# core.autocrlf=true on Windows rewrites text files to CRLF on checkout. For a shell script that is
# fatal in a way that reads as nonsense: the kernel takes the whole shebang line literally and the
# script dies with "bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env bash^M". android/gradlew was already checked out
# with CRLF, so the release-builder container (Dockerfile.android) could never have run it.
*.sh text eol=lf
gradlew text eol=lf
.githooks/* text eol=lf

# Read by the Docker daemon rather than by Windows.
Dockerfile text eol=lf
Dockerfile.* text eol=lf
docker-compose.yml text eol=lf

# cmd.exe misreads labels and GOTOs in a batch file with LF endings.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Never diffed or merged as text.
*.jks binary
*.keystore binary
*.apk binary
